      <!--
      GRIB feature collections for the ncar_3km_* series (CAPE, storm-relative helicity,
      etc). Without these, a request against a single file's dodsC/ncss endpoint makes
      TDS build an ephemeral single-file GRIB collection at request time where the 
      caches location does not allow enough space for it; for the
      gzipped era that path mishandles the .gz suffix: it looks for the decompressed
      "*.grb" name instead of the actual "*.grb.gz" file on disk, and fails with:
        IOException: java.lang.IllegalStateException: No files in this collection ...
      A managed featureCollection routes indexing through the TDM/GribIndex pipeline
      (same one used successfully by d113000, d626000, d335000, etc. above), which
      gives OPeNDAP/NCSS the same server-side per-variable subsetting already working
      for the diags_d02 netCDF files.

      Verified directly against /gdex/data/d300000 (2015-2017) on 2026-08-17:
        - Archive has two naming/format eras, split by SUFFIX not by a clean date
          cutoff:
            * "ncar_3km_<yyyyMMddHH>_mem<N>_f<NNN>.grb.gz" is GRIB EDITION 1
              (confirmed: wgrib2 reports "grib1 message ignored ... use wgrib" on a
              decompressed sample). Used 2015-04-07 (archive start) through
              2015-08-31, i.e. 2015-09-01 is the effective cutover to GRIB2.
            * "ncar_3km_<yyyyMMddHH>_mem<N>_f<NNN>.grb2" is GRIB EDITION 2 (confirmed
              via wgrib2). Exclusive format from 2015-09-01 onward (2015 rest of
              year, all of 2016, all of 2017 checked; no other extensions found).
            * Two anomalous transition-testing days, 2015-08-12 and 2015-08-29, each
              have a full normal .grb.gz set PLUS a partial duplicate .grb2 set for
              f000-f003 (all valid data, not corrupt); harmless overlap, no special
              casing needed since the suffix-based spec naturally separates them.
        - No ensemble/perturbation metadata in the GRIB PDS (wgrib2 -ens on a sample
          CAPE record returns nothing); member number exists ONLY in the filename.
          A single featureCollection spanning all 10 "mem*" files per era would give
          GRIB indistinguishable messages for the same parameter/level/valid-time and
          risk coordinate collisions, so each member gets its own featureCollection
          below (20 total: 10 members x 2 formats), mirroring how d113000/d626000
          split their series into several featureCollections.
        - CAPE and HLCY (1000-0m and 3000-0m layers) are both present in the GRIB2
          era sample; no field literally named "shear" was found in that file's
          inventory (may need a different field name/derivation; flag to Tom).

      -->

